-->
Comparator is a functional interface because it has only ONE abstract method: compare().
The equals() method in Comparator is NOT abstract — it comes from Object 

Why equals() Is NOT Abstract Here
Key Rule (Very Important)
Methods inherited from java.lang.Object do NOT count as abstract methods in a functional interface.


*
can 1 functional interface extends other functional interface
A functional interface can extend another functional interface as long as there is only one abstract method in the combined interface; 
default and static methods don’t count.

@FunctionalInterface
interface A {
    void m1();
}

@FunctionalInterface
interface B extends A {
    // No new abstract methods
    // Still functional
}

✅ B is functional because it only has one abstract method: m1().

but suppose there is abstract method m2() in B then we will get the error  because now there will be 2 abstract methods
then if we want to use interface then remove the @FunctionalInterface then it will act as normal interface


