whem to use map and flatmap

map() — one-to-one transformation
When to use it

Use map when:
Each input element is transformed into exactly one output element
You are changing the type or value, but not the structure

List<String> names = List.of("alice", "bob", "charlie");

List<String> upper =
    names.stream()
         .map(String::toUpperCase)
         .toList();
Input → Output
"alice"   → "ALICE"
"bob"     → "BOB"


flatMap() — one-to-many (or many-to-one) transformation
When to use it

Use flatMap when:
Each input element can produce 0, 1, or many output elements
You want to flatten nested structures
You want to avoid Stream<Stream<T>>

List<List<Integer>> lists = List.of(
    List.of(1, 2),
    List.of(3, 4),
    List.of(5)
);

❌ Using map (wrong for flattening)
Stream<Stream<Integer>> stream =
    lists.stream()
         .map(List::stream);

Result type:
Stream<Stream<Integer>>

✅ Using flatMap
List<Integer> flat =
    lists.stream()
         .flatMap(List::stream)
         .toList();

Result:
[1, 2, 3, 4, 5]


