In-memory operations

Rule

Thread pool size ≈ number of CPU cores

how to check CPU cores->
int cores = Runtime.getRuntime().availableProcessors();
ExecutorService pool = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(cores);

For CPU-bound tasks, thread pool size should be close to the number of available processors.”


I/O-bound tasks 🌐
Examples
Database calls
File read/write
Network calls
REST API calls
Waiting on locks

Rule
Thread pool size > number of CPU cores
Because:
Threads spend time waiting
While one waits, another can run

Famous formula (INTERVIEW GOLD 🏆)

From Java Concurrency in Practice:
Threads = CPU cores × (1 + Wait time / Compute time)
Example
CPU cores = 8
Task spends 80% waiting, 20% computing

Threads = 8 × (1 + 80/20)
        = 8 × (1 + 4)
        = 40 threads


