What is the difference between Stream.iterate() and Stream.generate()?

Stream.iterate() and Stream.generate() in Java 8 are both methods for creating infinite streams, but
they do so in different ways. Stream.iterate() takes a seed (initial value) and a function, applying the
function repeatedly to generate a sequence (e.g., creating a stream of powers of two).
Stream.generate(), on the other hand, uses a supplier to provide new values, which doesn't depend
on the previous element. This makes Stream.generate() suitable for generating streams where each
element is independent of the others.


static <T> Stream<T> iterate(T seed, UnaryOperator<T> f)

How It Works
    Starts with a seed value
    Each next element is derived from the previous one
    Stateful generation (depends on prior value)

Example

Stream.iterate(1, n -> n + 1)
      .limit(5)
      .forEach(System.out::println);

Output:
1 2 3 4 5

Typical Use Cases
    Sequences (numbers, dates)
    Mathematical progressions
    Pagination logic

3. Stream.generate()
Definition

Stream.generate() produces elements using a Supplier, independent of previous elements.
Method Signature

static <T> Stream<T> generate(Supplier<T> s)

How It Works
    Calls the supplier each time a new element is needed
    Stateless generation
    Values can be random or constant

Example

Stream.generate(Math::random)
      .limit(5)
      .forEach(System.out::println);

Typical Use Cases
    Random values
    UUID generation
    Constant streams
    Lazy object creation

Stream.iterate() generates elements based on the previous value, making it ideal for sequences, 
while Stream.generate() produces elements independently using a supplier, making it suitable for random or constant values.

11. How to Answer in Interview (Tip)

Start with:

    “Both are used to create infinite streams.”

End with:

    “The key difference is stateful vs stateless generation.”


